Instruction:
Write a C++ function that takes a reference to a std::string containing markdown formatted text and returns a std::string containing html formatted text.

Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\ Certainly! Below is a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. This function will use a simple conversion for demonstration purposes, but in a real-world scenario, you would use a library like `markdown-to-html` or `michelf/php-markdown`.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <iostream>

// Function to convert markdown to HTML
std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Simple conversion for demonstration purposes
    std::string html = markdown;
    // Example: Convert all markdown links to HTML links
    size_t pos = 0;
    while ((pos = html.find("(", pos)) != std::string::npos) {
        size_t end = html.find(")", pos);
        std::string link = html.substr(pos + 1, end - pos - 1);
        html.replace(pos, end - pos + 2, "<a href=\"" + link + "\">" + link + "</a>");
        pos = end + 1;
    }
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "Check out this link: (https://example.com)";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function is a simple example and does not cover all markdown features. For a more robust solution, consider using a library like `markdown-to-html` or `michelf/php-markdown`.

